For now, I've mostly used `transformData` to modify a specific attribute.
I think it makes more sense to pass a deep cloned version of the data object than the original one.

For instance, to make sure we're not messing with internal variables, for a price facet refinement, we'd have to do:

``` js
{
  transformData: function(data) {
    return {
      isRefined: data.isRefined,
      name: '$' + data.name,
      count: data.count,
      etc.
    }
  }
}
```

or

``` js
{
  transformData: function(data) {
    data = whatevercloningmethodwewant(data);
    data.name = '$' + data.name;
    return data;
  }
}
```

I'd like to easily be able to do:

``` js
{
  transformData: function(data) {
    data.name = '$' + data.name;
    return data;
  }
}
```

It would be less error prone, and probably not resource consuming since we're using the original data if `transformData` hasn't been specified.
I'm opening this issue since I've been affected in my `currentRefinedValues` implementation, I modified the `name` value for the display and ended up screwing up my `onClick` handler which was using it.
